                    <p class="eyebrow">Embedded Systems · Python · CAD</p>
                    <h1>PEBBLE</h1>
                    <p class="subtitle">Intelligent Study Companion</p>
                    <p class="hero-intro">I designed and built a Raspberry Pi desk companion that connects a study timer to motion and presence sensing, camera detection, voice interaction, and physical feedback.</p>
                    <div class="project-meta"><span>Duke University</span><span>Spring 2026</span><span>Individual course project</span></div>
                    <p class="status">Built and demonstrated · Hardware, software, and enclosure design</p>
                    <a class="text-link" href="#contributions">My role <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
                </div>
                <figure class="hero-media">
                    <a href="/images/projects/pebble/bench-prototype.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full PEBBLE bench prototype photograph"><img src="/images/projects/pebble/bench-prototype.jpg" alt="Powered PEBBLE bench prototype with Raspberry Pi, camera ribbon cable, buttons and LEDs, sensor boards, illuminated LCD, ultrasonic sensor, and fan peripherals" width="2048" height="1536" /></a>
                    <figcaption><strong>Powered bench prototype</strong><span>The connected hardware during development.</span></figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <nav class="page-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#contributions">My role</a><a href="#system">System</a><a href="#hardware">Hardware</a><a href="#enclosure">Enclosure</a><a href="#outcome">Results</a></nav>

            <section class="section split" id="overview">
                <div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>A Companion That Responds</h2></div>
                <div class="copy">
                    <p class="lead">Connect the study timer to what the hardware senses at the desk.</p>
                    <p>I built PEBBLE around a Raspberry Pi, an LCD, physical controls, and multiple sensors. The timer pauses when the control system classifies distraction or detects that the user has left the desk, then resumes when its conditions for study are met.</p>
                    <p>I started with the idea of a pet-rock-inspired desk companion and developed it into a study timer linked to sensed behavior. The faceted enclosure carries that original concept into the physical design.</p>
                    <p>I also integrated a voice assistant, reaction and memory games, buzzer melodies, and temperature-based fan control, all accessed through the device's physical interface.</p>
                    <div class="callout"><strong>Design focus: make sensing, controls, and feedback work together in one desk device.</strong><span>The motion labels and camera detections are proxies used by the prototype; they do not directly measure a person's attention.</span></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section dark" id="contributions">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>My Role</h2></div><p>I completed PEBBLE individually, handling the hardware, Python software, system integration, and enclosure CAD.</p></div>
                <div class="contribution-list">
                    <div><span>01</span><p>Built and wired the Raspberry Pi prototype, connecting the sensor boards, buttons, LEDs, LCD, and feedback peripherals.</p></div>
                    <div><span>02</span><p>Developed the Python timer and mode-control logic, combining motion classification, ultrasonic presence sensing, and camera detections with LCD, LED, and buzzer feedback.</p></div>
                    <div><span>03</span><p>Integrated the speech-recognition, language-model API, and speech-output components, along with games and temperature-based fan control.</p></div>
                    <div><span>04</span><p>Designed the faceted enclosure in CAD, using component measurements to plan the openings, internal space, cable access, and display and sensor placement.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="system">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03</p><h2>How the System Works</h2></div><p>My Python control loop connects sensor input to timer behavior, user feedback, and interactive modes.</p></div>
                <div class="architecture" role="group" aria-label="System architecture: inputs feed Raspberry Pi processing, which controls the outputs">
                    <article><p class="eyebrow">Inputs</p><h3>Sense the desk</h3><ul><li>MPU6050 motion sensor</li><li>Ultrasonic distance sensor</li><li>Camera detections</li><li>DHT11 temperature sensor</li><li>Buttons and USB microphone</li></ul></article>
                    <article class="processor"><p class="eyebrow">Raspberry Pi</p><h3>Interpret &amp; respond</h3><ul><li>Motion feature extraction</li><li>Decision-tree classification</li><li>Person and phone-object detection</li><li>Timer and mode control</li></ul><p class="api-note">Voice interaction: local speech recognition → language-model API → speech output.</p></article>
                    <article><p class="eyebrow">Outputs</p><h3>Make the state visible</h3><ul><li>16 × 2 LCD</li><li>Green, yellow, and red LEDs</li><li>Buzzer alerts and melodies</li><li>Spoken responses</li><li>Temperature-controlled fan</li></ul></article>
                </div>
                <div class="split control-study">
                    <div class="copy"><h3>From sensing to timer behavior</h3><p>The motion classifier uses acceleration and gyroscope features. Camera detections and ultrasonic distance contribute to the system's presence and engagement logic. Detected phone objects take priority over the normal study state.</p><p>The implementation smooths motion classifications across recent predictions and uses separate entry and exit distance thresholds for presence. These mechanisms reduce sensitivity to an isolated reading.</p></div>
                    <div class="state-table"><table><caption>Study timer states</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Control state</th><th scope="col">Timer response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row"><span class="state-dot green" aria-hidden="true"></span>Focused</th><td>Countdown enabled</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><span class="state-dot yellow" aria-hidden="true"></span>Distracted</th><td>Countdown paused</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><span class="state-dot red" aria-hidden="true"></span>Away</th><td>Countdown paused</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Phone detected</th><td>Pause and alert</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="media-note">States describe the prototype's control logic, rather than validated measures of study performance.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="mode-grid"><article><h3>Voice assistant</h3><p>Vosk transcribes microphone input, an API supplies a response, and Piper generates speech for playback.</p></article><article><h3>Interactive modes</h3><p>Buttons navigate the timer, assistant, reaction game, Simon-style memory game, and buzzer music modes.</p></article><article><h3>Fan control</h3><p>The temperature sensor feeds a PWM fan-control module with stepped speed commands.</p></article></div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="hardware">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04</p><h2>Building the Hardware</h2></div><p>I developed the hardware on the bench so I could adjust and debug the connections while bringing the sensors, interface, and outputs together.</p></div>
                <div class="hardware-copy"><h3>Integration across several interfaces</h3><p>The LCD and motion sensor used I²C, while buttons, indicators, distance sensing, and output drivers connected through GPIO. The camera used a ribbon connection to the Pi; microphone and speaker hardware supported the voice interface.</p><p>Developing on the bench kept individual connections available for adjustment. The enclosure design then had to accommodate the actual boards, connectors, cable paths, and outward-facing components.</p></div>
