            <section class="section" id="enclosure">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">05</p><h2>Designing Around the Components</h2></div><p>The pet-rock concept became a faceted enclosure, with separate upper and lower shell models and openings for the hardware.</p></div>
                <div class="decision-grid"><article><h3>Measure before modeling</h3><p>I recorded component dimensions by hand while planning the case. Those measurements gave the CAD a physical starting point: space for the prototype boards, access to the controls, and a visible LCD window.</p></article><article><h3>Connect the shell to the interfaces</h3><p>The upper shell carries the display and sensor openings, while the lower model forms a separate base. The CAD also includes perforated panels and a cable opening to accommodate the outward-facing hardware and connections.</p></article></div>
                <div class="measurements"><table><caption>Component measurements recorded during enclosure development</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Component</th><th scope="col">Recorded size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Mini breadboards (3)</th><td>34 × 45 × 10 mm each</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Buttons</th><td>10 × 10 × 5 mm</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">LCD visible display</th><td>63 × 15 mm</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="media-note">March 30 development notes also specify a planned 3 × 1 in LCD window. These are design inputs, rather than final enclosure tolerances.</p></div>
