            <section class="section dark split" id="outcome">
                <div><p class="eyebrow">06</p><h2>Results &amp; Lessons</h2></div>
                <div class="copy"><p class="lead">I built and demonstrated a Raspberry Pi companion that combined sensing, software, and a physical interface.</p><p>The final course prototype brought together the study timer, display, controls, audio, and sensor-driven behavior. I also completed the custom enclosure design. The photos show the bench hardware, while the model views document the case geometry.</p><h3>Design the hardware and software together</h3><p>Building both sides of the system meant debugging connections alongside the Python modules that used them. A sensor reading had to translate into consistent timer behavior and clear feedback on the LCD and indicators.</p><h3>Bring physical constraints into the CAD</h3><p>Connectors need clearance, the display needs visibility, sensors need outward-facing openings, and wiring needs a route through the enclosure. Measuring the components helped me account for those requirements in the shell design.</p><h3>Further development</h3><p>I would next evaluate the timer logic across different users and desk arrangements and measure how reliably it responds to sensor inputs. The course demonstration established the prototype's functionality; its effect on study habits has not been evaluated.</p></div>
            </section>

